// ─── style/vendor-component-containment ───────────────────────────────
//
// Tag:      style
// Mechanism: GritQL (per-file, real-time)
// Blocking: Yes
//
// Prevents: Feature code importing a UI-library component directly when
//           the project ships its own wrapper for it.
//
//           A wrapper exists because the raw component was missing a
//           convention every use site needs — Enter-to-submit on a
//           compose box, a loading state on every button, an analytics
//           id on every link. The wrapper holds that convention in one
//           place. An import that goes around it silently reintroduces a
//           component that hand-rolls the convention, or omits it, and
//           the two versions drift apart from there. Nothing about the
//           bypassing call site looks wrong, which is why this needs a
//           rule and not a review.
//
//           This is the design-system sibling of
//           `boundary/sdk-containment`: same containment shape, applied
//           to component libraries rather than service SDKs.
//
// Applies:  All source files EXCEPT:
//           - The wrapper module itself (it must import the original)
//           - Test files and scripts
//
// Error:    "Import <Component> from the app wrapper, not the library —
//            the wrapper carries the convention every use site needs."
//
// ── Adapt ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// 1. One rule per wrapped component, or one rule with an `or`:
//    The pattern below names a single library and a single component.
//    Wrapping several components from the same library is common — add
//    them to the identifier alternation in `wrapped_component_import()`
//    and generalise the message, or copy the file per component when the
//    messages need to say different things. Prefer separate rules when
//    the reasons differ; an agent fixes what the message explains.
//
// 2. Write the rule when you write the wrapper, not later:
//    A wrapper without this rule lasts about as long as the memory of
//    the person who wrote it. The bypass is invisible in review, so the
//    rule is the only thing that keeps the wrapper canonical.
//
// 3. Do not exempt "just this once":
//    If a call site genuinely needs the unwrapped component, that is
//    usually a missing prop on the wrapper. Add the prop. An exemption
//    list here becomes the second implementation the rule existed to
//    prevent.
//
// ── Implementation note ───────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// This rule matches the import with a REGEX, not with an
// `import $_ from $source` snippet, because Biome's GritQL matches
// named-import snippets by ARITY: the snippet matches
// `import { Textarea } from "@lib/core"` but NOT
// `import { Textarea, TextInput } from "@lib/core"`. A snippet-based
// version of this rule silently passes every multi-specifier import,
// which is the common shape — so it would look like it works while
// catching almost nothing. Verify this before writing any import-shaped
// rule of your own.
//
// Grit regexes are anchored (full match), hence the `(?s).*` … `.*`
// wrapping. `[^}]*` spans newlines, so a formatter-wrapped multi-line
// import still matches. The diagnostic is spanned on the module string
// literal so the arrow lands on the import.
//
// ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

language js(typescript, jsx)

// Adapt 1: the identifier alternation and the module name are the two things
// to change. `\b` around the identifier keeps `TextareaProps` from matching.
pattern wrapped_component_import() {
    r"(?s).*import\s*\{[^}]*\b(?:Textarea)\b[^}]*\}\s*from\s*\"@mantine/core\".*"
}

file(name=$filename, body=$program) where {
    $filename <: r".*/src/.*",

    // --- Global exclusions (shared across all rules) ---
    ! $filename <: r".*\.test\.[tj]sx?$|.*\.integration\.test\.[tj]sx?$|.*__tests__.*|.*src/test/.*",
    ! $filename <: r".*/scripts/.*",

    // --- The wrapper module must import the original (Adapt 1) ---
    ! $filename <: r".*/src/shared/ui/textarea\.tsx$",

    $program <: wrapped_component_import(),
    $program <: contains bubble($program) r"\"@mantine/core\"" as $source where {
        register_diagnostic(
            span = $source,
            message = "Import Textarea from @/shared/ui/textarea, not @mantine/core. The app wrapper carries the convention every compose box shares (Enter submits, Shift+Enter inserts a newline, via onEnter); importing the library component directly reintroduces a field that hand-rolls or omits it. Only the wrapper itself may import the original. See docs/architecture/design-system.md."
        )
    }
}
